{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations of Isaac, Abraham's son</b> — concerning Esau and Jacob his sons, who are mentioned below, and Scripture relates how their birth came about." "<b>Abraham begot Isaac</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 25:19): since it is written \"Isaac the son of Abraham,\" it was necessary to say \"Abraham begot Isaac,\" because the scoffers of the generation were saying that Sarah conceived from Abimelech. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, shaped his features to resemble Abraham's, so that all would say, \"Abraham begot Isaac.\" And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 25:19) said further that the meaning of \"begot\" (הוֹלִיד) is \"raised\" and \"brought up,\" as in \"were born upon Joseph's knees\" (Genesis 50:23), just as he said, \"and he sent them away from upon Isaac his son\" (ibid.).\n\nBut the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he now went back and began with the head of the genealogy, according to the practice of Scripture to return, in the case of men of distinction, to the head of the genealogy. So too in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 17:18) it is written, \"The sons of Shem: Elam, and Asshur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram; and Arphaxad begot Shelah,\" and when he finished he went back and said (ibid. 24), \"Shem, Arphaxad, Shelah,\" up to \"Abram, the same is Abraham.\" And so in the genealogy of Benjamin he went back and began, \"and Ner begot Kish, and Kish begot Saul\" (1 Chronicles 8:33; 9:39). So too here: Abraham begot Isaac, and Isaac begot Jacob, as he will mention.\n\nAnd he went back to recount this because he had said (Genesis Rabbah 62:1), \"And these are the generations of Ishmael, Abraham's son\" — for if he had said merely \"And these are the generations of Isaac, Abraham's son,\" he would have equated the two of them in lineage and in standing, even though he gave precedence to the firstborn. Moreover, it would have been proper to begin with Abraham and say, \"These are the generations of Abraham,\" but he did not wish to do so, so as not to enumerate Ishmael and the sons of Keturah. Therefore he went back and completed, \"Abraham begot Isaac,\" to say that he alone is his generations, and it is as though he begot no other who is reckoned, as it is said to him (Genesis 21:12), \"for in Isaac shall seed be called to you.\" And therefore he said above (Genesis 25:12) as well, \"whom Hagar the Egyptian, Sarah's handmaid, bore to Abraham,\" for Isaac's honor, to say that these are not the generations of the lineage of Abraham but rather they are the sons of the maidservant, as he said (Genesis 21:13), \"and also the son of the maidservant I will make a nation.\" And so Scripture did likewise in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 1:28), for he said at first, \"The sons of Abraham: Isaac and Ishmael, and these are their generations: the firstborn of Ishmael, Nebaioth,\" and he mentioned \"and the sons of Keturah, Abraham's concubine, who bore Zimran,\" and it would have been proper to say \"the sons of Isaac,\" but he went back and began, \"and Abraham begot Isaac; the sons of Isaac: Esau and Israel.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The phrase צָר הקב\"ה קְלַסְתֵּר פָּנָיו is rendered literally as \"shaped his features\"; the idiom refers to forming the child's facial appearance. In the final sentence, the pronoun 'he' refers to the author of Chronicles."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:58.886062873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}